<section>
<h1>Windows App Development</h1>

<p>
    I&rsquo;m a Windows developer based in New Zealand. I&rsquo;ve spent many years building
    desktop apps in C# and XAML, and I take on contract work alongside my day job.
</p>

</section>
<section>
<h2>Availability</h2>
<p>
    I take on a small amount of contract work alongside my role at <a href="https://mu.se/">Muse Group</a>,
    where I lead the Windows and Linux clients for <a href="https://www.musehub.com/">MuseHub</a>.
    I&rsquo;ve worked remotely for years, so timezones generally aren&rsquo;t a problem.
    I&rsquo;m in New Zealand (UTC+12/+13).
</p>
<p>
    If you&rsquo;ve got something that fits the work below, <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>.
</p>

</section>
<section>
    <h2>What I work on</h2>

        <h3>Desktop apps in C# and XAML</h3>
        <p>
            <abbr title="Windows App SDK">WinUI</abbr>, <abbr title="Windows Presentation Foundation">WPF</abbr>,
            <abbr title="Universal Windows Platform">UWP</abbr> and Avalonia. New apps, or features
            and fixes on existing ones. I&rsquo;m comfortable in the parts of the Windows App SDK
            that aren&rsquo;t documented well: custom title bars and window management, unpackaged
            and self contained deployment, app lifecycle and single instancing, and dropping down to
            WinRT interop when the C# API doesn&rsquo;t go far enough.
        </p>

        <h3>Migrations and upgrades</h3>
        <p>
            Moving WPF or UWP apps onto WinUI, or onto Avalonia if you need Linux and macOS as well.
            Mostly this is mapping controls that have no direct equivalent, sorting out the bits of
            the dispatcher, imaging and resource systems that changed, and untangling
            <code>DynamicResource</code> theming. Happy to do the migration itself, or just look
            over the codebase and tell you what it&rsquo;d take.
        </p>

        <h3>XAML from design</h3>
        <p>
            Turning a design into XAML that actually matches it. That means spacing, typography,
            Light, Dark and High Contrast themes, sensible behaviour at different window sizes, and
            keyboard and screen reader support. This is most of what I did at StaffPad and Muse.
            It&rsquo;s usually more work than people expect.
        </p>

        <h3>Native C++ on Windows</h3>
        <p>
            I work in the shared C++ core at Muse alongside the C# front end, and have written a
            voxel engine and a few graphics experiments in my own time. Happy to take on native
            Windows work, or the C++ side of an app that has both.
        </p>

        <h3>Packaging and distribution</h3>
        <p>
            <abbr title="MSIX packaging format">MSIX</abbr>, self contained Windows App SDK
            deployment, code signing, installers, auto update and Store submission. Also the odd
            problems that only turn up in production, mark of the web on downloaded installers
            being the usual one.
        </p>

        <h3>Performance and debugging</h3>
        <p>
            Apps that are slow, leaking, or only crashing on someone else&rsquo;s machine. XAML
            layout and virtualisation, startup time, memory, and getting crash reports that
            actually tell you something.
        </p>

</section>
<section>
    <h2>Background</h2>

    <p>
        I lead the Windows and Linux clients for <a href="https://www.musehub.com/">MuseHub</a> at
        <a href="https://mu.se/">Muse Group</a>, and also contribute to the shared C++ core and the
        TypeScript backend. Before that I spent four years on the Windows client for
        <a href="https://www.staffpad.net/">StaffPad</a>, and built industrial line of business
        systems for <a href="https://www.inex.co.nz/">Independent Extrusions</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Outside of XAML I work in C++, TypeScript and Rust, and spend spare time on low level
        graphics and embedded programming. That tends to help when a Windows problem turns out to
        be a native one.
    </p>

    <p>
        There&rsquo;s more on my <a href="/projects">projects page</a> and my
